201~200~The project is the major assessment item for this course, and is divided into a team-based project
implementation in which students work in teams of 4–5 to build and evaluate a recommender
system, and an individual report in which students outline the recommender system and summarize
the results of the project, reflect on the whole process, and propose future directions for the work
in the light of material presented in the lectures in the second half of the course.
The project is expected to follow (and build on) an initial project design, and as such should
focus on all aspects of building and evaluating a recommender system – the scope, dataset(s),
method(s) and evaluation in the design (metrics and perhaps a small user study). Consider also
the justification for your final design choices, such as why one particular style of recommender
system is most suitable for the chosen scenario, or why one dataset is more useful than another.


Implementation

The project implementation should follow the style of the assignment and include the content
outlined in the project design (possibly refined), now with experimental results for the different
methods. Your team should include one (or more) Jupyter notebooks that show your reasoning
process through the construction and evaluation of your recommendation models, including intermediate evaluations that went into the decision of the final recommender system proposal. Place
auxiliary software modules in separate files, and do not submit the dataset(s).

The mark for the project implementation is determined from a presentation held in Week 10, and
is based on the sophistication of the recommender system, the thoroughness of the evaluation, the
interpretation of the results, and the extent to which the project achieved its objectives.

Each member of a team generally receives the same mark for the project implementation, however marks are moderated according to individual contributions determined
using peer assessments: in “dysfunctional teams” where member contributions are
highly unequal, marks will be adjusted to reflect the level of contribution. The Moodle
Team Evaluation tool will be used for each team member to assess (anonymously) the relative
contributions of the team members. Students are required to enter their peer assessments using
the tool by 5:00 p.m. the day before the first project presentations. Once the due date has passed,
these percentages cannot be modified.

Presentation

Each team will present a 20-minute summary of their project and findings at a time to be arranged
in Week 10. The structure of the presentation should follow that of the results part of the report
(see below) and cover the project scope, dataset(s), method(s) developed, and evaluations of the
methods and proposed recommender system using historical data and/or any user evaluation,
including the rationale for any design decisions. It is recognized that there may not be enough
time for every detail to be included in the presentation, however additional information can be
included in the report. Note that in constrast to the project pitch, your audience is mainly
interested in results and outcomes. Leave time for audience questions.
As the presentations form part of the assessment for the project, only the project team and markers
will be present. Your presentation should be in PowerPoint or pdf, and include a description of
the problem, sufficiently detailed experimental results, interpretations of the results, and final
conclusions. Include a copy of your team presentation with your project submission.
